---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { getPublishedSoftware } from "@/data/software";

const products = getPublishedSoftware();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security — BramLabs"
  description="How to report a security vulnerability in a BramLabs product, what happens next, and what is in scope."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="page-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Vulnerability disclosure</p>
        <h1 class="h-page">Reporting a security issue</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          If you have found a vulnerability in a BramLabs product or in
          bramlabs.co, report it privately and it will be looked at. There is no
          bug bounty programme — but there is a real person who reads the inbox.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>How to report</h2>
        <p>
          Email <a href="mailto:support@bramlabs.co">support@bramlabs.co</a> with
          "Security" in the subject line. Include:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Which product and version is affected</li>
          <li>The steps to reproduce it, in enough detail to follow</li>
          <li>What an attacker could actually do with it</li>
          <li>Any proof-of-concept — as an attachment, not a public link</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Please do not open a public issue, post details on social media, or
          include them in a store review before the issue is resolved.
        </p>

        <h2>What happens next</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Acknowledgement within 5 business days.</strong> If you have not
            heard back by then, resend — assume the mail was lost rather than
            ignored.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Assessment within 10 business days,</strong> with a
            reproduction attempt and a severity judgement shared with you.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A fix, or a written explanation of why not.</strong> Timelines
            depend on severity and on store review queues, which are outside
            BramLabs' control.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Credit, if you want it.</strong> Say so in your report and
            you will be named in the release notes.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>In scope</h2>
        <ul>
          {
            products.map((p) => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/software/${p.slug}/`}>{p.title}</a> — {p.platform}
              </li>
            ))
          }
          <li>bramlabs.co, including the contact and feedback endpoints</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Out of scope</h2>
        <p>
          These are known, accepted, or not something BramLabs can act on:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Reports from automated scanners with no demonstrated exploitability
          </li>
          <li>
            Missing security headers on pages that carry no user data, absent a
            concrete attack
          </li>
          <li>
            Denial of service, volumetric testing, or anything that degrades the
            service for other people
          </li>
          <li>
            Social engineering of BramLabs, its users, or store review staff
          </li>
          <li>
            Vulnerabilities in third-party platforms themselves — report those to
            Amazon, Google or Cloudflare directly
          </li>
          <li>
            Issues requiring a physically compromised or rooted device, or an
            attacker who already has the victim's credentials
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Safe harbour</h2>
        <p>
          Research conducted in good faith under this policy is welcome. If you
          stay within scope, avoid accessing or modifying other people's data,
          avoid degrading the service, and give a reasonable window before public
          disclosure, BramLabs will not pursue or support legal action against
          you for it.
        </p>

        <h2>Machine-readable policy</h2>
        <p>
          The same contact details are published at
          <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">/.well-known/security.txt</a>
          following RFC 9116.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style lang="scss">
  .prose ol {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.55rem;
  }
</style>
